# Module: feature-registry — the feature inventory

The capability inventory of **{{ project_name }}**: what can the product do,
where is that proven, and which behaviour is an addressed target? One entry per
capability (a story), each tracing to its acceptance items and the tests that
prove them. Runs via `process-gates` (hosted CI with `ci.github: true`, and the
pre-push hook) and locally via `python scripts/process/check_feature_registry.py`.

## Proven behaviour and addressable targets — not a work log

Two questions used to share this registry; the lean pass split them:

- **"What is in flight?"** is the work log — owned by **GitHub
  Issues/Sub-Issues/Projects** (status labels, `blocked_by` issue links,
  board columns). The retired fields `status`, `blocked_by`, and `parent` are
  **hard failures** here — a leftover is stale double bookkeeping (a project
  that keeps a story status declares it, see Configuration).
- **"What does the product do, and what is it meant to do next?"** is this
  inventory. It holds **proven behaviour and addressable targets**: every
  acceptance item has an id and is referred to as `<story id>#<item id>`
  (`STORY-0007#AC3`). An item is `proven` by default; an item marked
  `"state": "target"` states behaviour that does not exist yet and names the
  issue that delivers it. The work on a target — its plan, its progress, its
  discussion — stays in that issue; the registry only makes the target
  addressable, so a plan, a design round or a test can point at it before it
  exists.

A hundred closed issues answer "what happened", not "what is": capabilities span
many issues and get reshaped by later ones. The inventory scales with
capabilities (dozens, slowly), not work items (hundreds, fast) — it stays
readable. It is fed at merge: a feature's spec user stories are promoted here
with their test references — the durable yield of the pruned spec artifacts
(see the speckit module's merge ritual, where installed).

## One entry per file

In the template's layout, create `docs/process/feature-registry/STORY-NNNN.json`
(one file per entry so entries diff and merge independently):

- **id** — `STORY-NNNN` (four digits), unique across the inventory.
- **title** — a short label.
- **story** — one sentence in EARS form: *As a `<role>`, when `<trigger>`,
  the system shall `<response>`.* Wording is graded by humans, not the gate.
- **acceptance** — a non-empty list of items (below).
- **tests** — a **non-empty** list of test refs that prove the entry; at least
  one names an existing test. An inventory entry without a resolvable test is a
  claim, not an inventory item.
- **adr** (optional) — `ADR-NNNN` linking a decision under
  `docs/process/adr/`.
- **issue** (optional) — provenance: the issue or PR that delivered the
  capability, as `#N`, `owner/repo#N` or a GitHub issue URL.
- **links** (optional) — free-form coupling notes (consumed contracts, pins).

A retired capability is **deleted** (git history is the archive) — the
inventory lists what exists or is addressed, not what once was.

## Acceptance items

Each item is an object; only `id` and `text` are required, every other field is
checked once it is there:

- **id** (required) — `AC<n>` (`^AC[1-9]\d*$`), unique within the story. It is
  assigned once and never renumbered: references (`STORY-0007#AC3`), plans,
  test names and review findings carry it. A removed item's id is not reused:
  it moves to the story's `retired` list (Stable ids, below).
- **text** (required) — the behaviour, by the writing rule below.
- **surfaces** — the surfaces (platforms) the item applies to: a subset of the
  story's surfaces, or of the project's declared `surfaces` where the story has
  none. Without the field the item applies to all of them. A project that
  declares no surfaces gets a note asking to declare them, and the field is not
  checked; its items have **one implicit surface**.
- **state** — `"proven"` (the default) or `"target"`, or a map from the item's
  surfaces to one of them (`{"web": "proven", "ios": "target"}`). An item with
  one implicit surface takes a single value. A target on any surface needs an
  **issue** on the item.
- **tests** — a map from the item's surfaces (and its `owned_by` layer) to test
  refs: `{"web": ["…"], "ios": ["…"]}`. Each surface is proven by the tests under
  its own key. A plain list is shorthand for an item with exactly one surface,
  the implicit one included; on an item with more surfaces it fails, so one
  test cannot pass for several surfaces at once.
- **owned_by** — one of the project's `shared_proof_layers` (e.g. `backend`):
  behaviour a shared layer owns for every surface of the item, proven by the
  tests under that layer's key. A shared-layer key in `tests` without the
  matching `owned_by` fails — a backend test does not stand in for client
  behaviour. Whether a behaviour really belongs to the shared layer stays review
  judgment; the gate's report states the share of `owned_by` items per
  capability, so client items drifting into a shared layer show in the number.
- **design** — the item's anchor in a design round. Accepted, not yet checked:
  a later template release fixes the anchor format and checks it.

`strict_item_tests` decides what an item without an existing test is: a failure
when on, otherwise one note per capability (or for the inventory) that counts
them. The checker proves that a test file and its `::node` exist, not that the
test tests the item — that stays review judgment.

## Writing rule and split rule

- **One behaviour per item, in the present tense.** `text` states what the
  system does — for a proven item and for a target alike. Whether the behaviour
  exists yet is said only by `state`.
- **No history, plans or issue numbers in `text`.** "No longer", "replaces",
  "since #123" and "will" belong in the issue or the spec; the item's issue goes
  in its `issue` field, the story's issues in the story's field.
- **No surface prefix in `text`.** "Web: …" becomes `"surfaces": ["web"]`.
- **Removing a behaviour removes its item and its tests in the same change**,
  and retires its id: the id goes into the story's `retired` list and is never
  given to another item.
- **Split rule.** A behaviour with a part a shared layer owns and a part each
  client shows is split: one item `owned_by` the shared layer (its proof under
  that layer's key), and one client item for each display, each proven on its
  own surface. No item mixes both — otherwise a server test would prove a
  client's behaviour.
- **Splitting a long item.** The part that keeps the original main behaviour
  keeps the old id; the parts split off get new ids, so existing references
  stay valid. Where no part keeps the main behaviour, the old id is retired with
  `split_into` naming the new ones.

## Stable ids

An item id is an address: plans, tests, design rounds and review findings cite
`STORY-0007#AC3`, so the id must keep naming the same behaviour. The gate holds
it so with `stable_ids` (on in the template).

**Retired ids.** A story lists the ids it removed:

```json
"retired": [{"id": "AC9"}, {"id": "AC12", "split_into": ["AC31", "AC32"]}]
```

Each `id` is an item id, stands once and is no longer an acceptance item.
`split_into` (optional) names the items of the same story that took the
behaviour over; none of them is retired. The form is always checked.

**Compared with the fork point.** The gate reads the registry as it stood at the
branch's fork point from the integration branch — the tip of `origin/main` for
the pre-push, the train and `finish`; PR CI through its merge commit — and
compares each story that exists there and today. Each of these fails, ids
compared by number (`AC10` > `AC9`):

- an id of the base is gone and not in `retired`;
- a `retired` entry of the base is gone;
- a new id is not higher than every id the base holds in `acceptance` and
  `retired`;
- **renumbered:** an item's text (whitespace aside) now stands under another id
  that did not carry it at the base, and that id is not in the old item's
  `split_into`. Moving AC4…AC10 down to AC3…AC9 is caught this way, even with
  `retired: AC10`. Two base items with one text, a copied item and a split
  target keeping the old wording are no renumbering. The rule sees wording that
  stayed literally the same: a changed comma escapes it. Wording handed to
  another id on purpose is retired with `split_into`.

A story is found by its `id`; one whose id the base does not hold is looked up
by its path, so a story renamed in the flat layout stays compared. In the
capabilities layout the file name is the id: a renamed story there is a deleted
one plus a new one. A new story is checked for the form only; deleting a whole
story is allowed (history is the archive) — refs to it are caught below.

Only `root`, `layout`, `id_pattern` and `stable_ids` of the base's configuration
count. Where there is no base to compare with, the gate says why in a note and
checks this tree alone: no fork point (on the integration branch; a shallow
clone, with `git fetch --unshallow` as the remedy; a remote without the fork),
an ambiguous one (a criss-cross merge), or no base registry (no configuration at
the base, as for a project adopting the registry; one that cannot be read or is
invalid; no registry directory there; a symlink, a submodule or a file that is
no UTF-8 in it).

**Refs inside the registry.** Every `<story id>#AC<n>` in a text value of a story
or capability head — test refs included — must name an item of today's
registry. A ref to a retired id with `split_into` is a note naming the
successors; one to a retired id without it, to an unknown story or to an unknown
id fails. A URL anchor (`…/spec#AC4`) without a story id in front is no ref.
Refs outside the registry are not checked here.

**Switching it off.** `"stable_ids": false` in `feature-registry.local.json`
switches the comparison and the ref check off — only where the base **and** the
branch say false, so a branch cannot lift the duty for itself. A base value that
is no boolean counts as true, with a note. The form of `retired` and
`--assign-ids` hold either way. Set on a branch, the switch takes effect once
that branch is merged.

## Test refs

A test ref is a repository path, optionally with a `::node` selector:
`tests/<area>/test_<x>.py::<Class>::<test>`, or `web/<x>.spec.ts::<test title>`.

- The file must exist (hard).
- Each `::` segment is checked: in a `.py` file against the functions and
  classes it defines (read by AST, never imported), in any other file as a
  substring. Whether a dead node is a note or a failure is `node_check`
  (default: note); an unparsable `.py` file counts the same way.
- With `test_ref_roots`, every ref starts with one of them.
- With `planned_refs`, a ref may be a placeholder `planned:#<issue>:<text>` for
  a test that does not exist yet. A placeholder is never proof: an entry whose
  `tests` are all placeholders fails, and an item with only placeholders counts
  as untested.

## Configuration

How the registry is laid out and which rules bind is configuration, read by
`scripts/process/registry_config.py` — the one owner of configuration and
loader for every gate that reads the registry. `docs/process/feature-registry.json`
is the template's file: each key's meaning stands in its `_comment`, and its
values are the template's behaviour described above. Unknown keys — also nested
ones, such as `issues.requird` — fail the registry gate, so a typo cannot drop a
rule silently; `_comment` is allowed at any level.

| Key | Template value | What it decides |
|---|---|---|
| `layout` | `flat` | `flat`: one file per story in `root`. `capabilities`: a directory per capability with a head `_capability.json` and one `<id>.json` per story. |
| `root` | `docs/process/feature-registry` | where the registry lives |
| `id_pattern` | `^STORY-\d{4}$` | a story id; `{prefix}` is the capability's prefix (capabilities layout) |
| `require_registry` | `false` | an absent registry, no capability or an empty capability fails instead of being a note |
| `story_text_fields` | `title`, `story` | story fields that must be non-empty text |
| `story_surfaces`, `surfaces` | `none`, `[]` | whether a story carries `surfaces` (`optional`, `required`), and the declared surfaces |
| `shared_proof_layers` | `[]` | layers an item may be `owned_by` |
| `story_tests` | `tests` | `tests` (a list) or `test_mapping` (a map from surface or shared layer to a list) |
| `issues` | `issue`, not required | the story's issue field (`issue` or a list `issues`), whether it is required, and which status needs a ref (`required_for`) |
| `story_status` | `null` | `null`: `status` stays retired. An object: `status` is required, one of `values`; a `deferred` story may leave a test-mapping list empty |
| `test_ref_roots`, `planned_refs`, `node_check` | `[]`, `false`, `note` | test refs, above |
| `retired_fields` | `status`, `blocked_by`, `parent` | fields that fail as retired (`status` drops out with `story_status`) |
| `extra_fields` | `[]` | further story fields the project knows; other unknown fields are a note |
| `strict_item_tests` | `false` | an item without an existing test fails |
| `stable_ids` | `true` | item ids are compared with the fork point and registry refs must resolve (Stable ids); off only where base and branch say `false` |

**The capabilities layout.** Each capability is a directory under `root` with a
head `_capability.json`: `schema_version` 1, `capability` equal to the
directory name, and `prefix` (`^[A-Z]+$`) when `id_pattern` uses `{prefix}`.
Further head fields are the project's. A story file is named after its id, an id
carries its capability's prefix and is unique across the registry, and JSON
directly in `root` fails (except `*.example.json`). The prefix is data, not
configuration: a new capability changes only its own head.

Who else reads the registry, through the same module: the product frame
(`check_product_frame.py`, a story reference in `PRODUCT.md` must resolve;
a reference in the loose form of `id_pattern` that the strict pattern refuses
is a note), the github-issues gate (where installed) and the plan-structure
gate, which binds story ids in new plans to the registry once a project file
stands at the integration ref (`journal-state-plans.md`).

## Overlay: the project's choices

A project writes its choices into `docs/process/feature-registry.local.json`;
the template never ships, renders or overwrites it. It is laid over the
template file: mappings merge key by key, everything else — lists included —
replaces whole, so a project's `retired_fields` list is the whole list. For
example, a registry with capability directories, per-capability prefixes, a
story status and a test mapping:

```json
{
  "_comment": "Capabilities with a status and a test mapping per surface.",
  "layout": "capabilities",
  "root": "docs/feature-registry",
  "id_pattern": "^{prefix}-US-\\d{3}$",
  "require_registry": true,
  "story_surfaces": "required",
  "surfaces": ["web", "ios", "android"],
  "shared_proof_layers": ["backend"],
  "story_tests": "test_mapping",
  "issues": {"field": "issues", "required": true, "required_for": "covered"},
  "story_status": {"values": ["covered", "partial", "planned", "deferred"],
                   "deferred": "deferred", "deferred_allows_empty_tests": true},
  "retired_fields": ["blocked_by", "parent"],
  "extra_fields": ["notes"]
}
```

Both files are read from the working tree, like `gates.local.json`, and both are
**gate configuration** (`check_review.GATE_FILES`): a change to either owes a
refute, and a plan that names one needs a cross-model plan review. The
plan-structure gate reads them at the integration ref instead, so a branch
cannot set the pattern its own plan is checked against.

## Migration

The item ids are a tightening without a project choice: an item without `id`
fails. Give every item one, once:

```
python scripts/process/check_feature_registry.py --assign-ids          # preview
python scripts/process/check_feature_registry.py --assign-ids --write
```

- In a story without any id or `retired` entry, a string item and an object
  without `id` become `{"id": "AC<n>", "text": …}`, `n` being its position from
  1 — an old positional reference "item 9 of STORY-0007" becomes
  `STORY-0007#AC9` without lookup.
- In a story that holds an id or a `retired` entry, every new item gets the
  highest id plus one — over today's ids and retired ids and, where the fork
  point's registry can be read, over the base's too, so an id removed on the
  branch is not given again. Where the fork point cannot be told, a note says so
  and the run numbers from this tree.
- An existing id that does not match `^AC[1-9]\d*$` is reported, never
  rewritten (references may name it), and the run exits 1.
- A second run changes nothing. The file keeps its indentation, non-ASCII text
  stays as written, and it ends with a newline. Both layouts migrate.

Then, per area and reviewed: move surface prefixes from `text` into `surfaces`;
apply the writing rule (issue references to the issue fields, history and plans
to issues or specs, long items split); add item `tests` as items are touched;
switch `strict_item_tests` on once coverage allows. A project that adds item
`surfaces` or `tests` must declare its `surfaces`; without them its items keep
one implicit surface and stay green.

## Hard vs. best-effort (no false-green)

**Hard (CI fails):** an unusable or unknown configuration key; invalid JSON;
missing/duplicate/misshaped story `id`; a missing text field; empty
`acceptance`; an item without `id`, with a misshaped or duplicate id, or
without text; an item field outside its rules (surfaces, state, a target
without an issue, tests, `owned_by`); a malformed `retired` list; with
`stable_ids`, an id changed against the fork point and a registry ref that
names no item, or a retired one without `split_into` (Stable ids); story tests that are empty, all
placeholders or name a path that does not exist; a retired field; a dangling
`adr` link; a malformed issue ref; with a project configuration, its rules
(layout, heads, prefixes, surfaces, status, test mapping, required issues).

**Best-effort (note, never fails):** an empty inventory ("no entries yet");
no base to compare item ids with, and why; a ref to a retired id with
`split_into`;
unknown story or item fields; a dead `::node` (unless `node_check` is `hard`);
items without an existing test (unless `strict_item_tests`); item surfaces
without declared project surfaces; the under-granularity advisory (an entry
with ≤2 criteria but ≥5 mapped tests likely under-describes its tested
behaviour). Whether a test *semantically* proves its criterion is review
judgment (`review-checklist.md`, Tests prove acceptance) — the gate never fakes
that.

**One owner per criterion.** A criterion's wording lives in one place at a
time. While the work is in flight, the spec owns it (`specs/<dir>/spec.md`,
or the issue where no spec is written). The change that delivers the
capability writes the inventory entry from the spec's final `AC-n` lines,
as its last step before the review bundle; from the merge on, the inventory
owns the wording and the spec is pruned (the speckit merge ritual, where
installed) or is history. A target item is the exception that proves the rule:
its wording is the registry's from the start, and its issue owns the work.
Plans, tasks, tests and reports refer to a criterion by its id and never
restate its text, so there is no second copy to drift. A later change to a
shipped criterion edits the inventory entry. Whether the entry matches the
spec it was promoted from is checked by the review, which sees both in the
same diff (`review-checklist.md`).
